# Zintriq project rules

## Host (do not replace)

- The existing Electron shell stays in `desktop/`. The existing Flask host stays `app.py`.
- The typed MVP UI is `apps/desktop` (Tauri + React). The typed MVP API is `apps/core-service` (Fastify on 127.0.0.1:4317). Do not fold those into the Electron main process.
- New domain code lives in `packages/*` (TypeScript, strict). Flask or Electron may call those packages; do not fold extension execution into the UI process.

## Product boundaries

- No proactive AI suggestions. No AI completion and no automatic code generation in this MVP. Slow work runs only after an explicit user action.
- Language support goes through an LSP/DAP client. Do not write a full language server. First clients are C/C++ and Python. A mock server is allowed if the client interface stays stable.
- Extensions run in a WASM sandbox. Each extension manifest declares capabilities (`fs.read`, `fs.write`, `net`, `serial`, `process`). Undeclared capabilities are unavailable. No unsandboxed process escape hatch.
- Keep UI, core service, and extension execution in separate processes.
- Boards come from declarative HAL YAML: MCU family, toolchain command, pins, peripherals. Ship ESP32, STM32, and RP2040 fixtures.
- Build check runs only when the user asks. It calls local g++/gcc and returns diagnostics. No flashing and no hardware-in-the-loop run in this MVP.
- TypeScript is strict. Validate boundaries with Zod. Return errors explicitly; do not swallow exceptions. Core modules need Vitest tests.
- Do not add a source-to-target language converter, its routes, buttons, types, or docs that promise it. A later ADR can decide; this MVP does not stub that feature.

## Coding

- Do not auto-build on save. Do not build when a file changes.
- `apps/desktop` calls only the core-service HTTP API. It does not import extension-host, build-service, or hal-registry.
- Do not put extension execution inside the Electron main process or the React UI.
- Offline mocks must pass tests. Do not require a paid cloud account to run.
- Prefer the existing editor UI over a second React app.
